  { time: '09:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 09:30', activity: 'Opening Ceremony', description: 'Welcome by General Chair and PC Chairs ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â Edward Hermann Haeusler ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â· GenaÃƒÆ’Ã†â€™Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â­na Nunes Rodrigues ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â· Pedro Ribeiro', type: 'ceremony' },
  { time: '09:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 10:30', activity: 'Keynote', title: 'Prof. Ana Cavalcanti', affiliation: 'University of York, UK', abstractTitle: 'Efficient and effective testing of cyclic systems', abstract: 'The RoboStar technology provides unique comprehensive support for design and verification of software for robots. The approach is model-centric: diagrams and (controlled) natural language are used to capture several aspects of a robotic system, and formal semantics, automatically generated, underpins techniques for simulation, proof, testing, and deployment. In this seminar, we present a practical model-based testing technique for RoboSim, the RoboStar notation that adopts the cyclic paradigm of simulations and of the cyclic executive programming pattern, for example. To enable generation of practical test sets, we use the timed testing theory for the process algebra CSP with inputs and outputs. We, however, for practical reasons, identify the test cases that become useless due to the specificities of cyclic mechanisms, and can be discarded. Secondly, we provide a technique to combine test cases, proving that soundness and completeness are preserved. Finally, we use the test cases to generate test drivers for C++ implementations of a collection of examples modelled in RoboSim. Our experiments show significant gains in efficiency. This state-of-the-art approach can significantly enhance the state-of-the-practice of testing in robotics.', type: 'keynote' },
  { time: '10:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 11:00', activity: 'Coffee Break', type: 'break' },
  { time: '11:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 12:30', activity: 'Session 1 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â Mechanized Proofs and Language Theory', chair: 'To be assigned', type: 'session' },
  { time: '11:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 11:30', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 9', title: 'Mechanizing a Resource-Aware Hoare Logic with Asymptotic Cost Assertions in Lean', authors: 'Gabriel AraÃƒÆ’Ã†â€™Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Âºjo Saldanha, Rodrigo Ribeiro', abstract: 'Reasoning about the cost of a program is central to computer science, yet the operation counts behind a complexity bound are usually established informally. Resource-aware Hoare logics internalize this reasoning through cost-annotated triples {P} S {Q | t}, but their metatheory typically rests only on pen-and-paper proofs. In this work, we present a mechanization in Lean 4 of one such logic, covering its cost-instrumented semantics, its cost logic and its verification condition generator, together with a relative-completeness result absent from the source. The mechanization exposed two errors in the source, which we correct: an unsound while rule whose iteration bound is off by one, and a primitive for semantics inconsistent with the cost its own rule charges. We further add elementary asymptotic (O) cost assertions at the level of triples, as derivable weakenings of the concrete bounds, and use them on two examples.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '11:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 12:00', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 10', title: 'A Machine-Checked Formalization of the Pumping Lemma for Context-Free Languages via Pushdown Automata', authors: 'Rodrigo Ribeiro', abstract: 'We present a machine-checked formalization, in the dependently typed language Agda, of the pumping lemma for context-free languages by the pushdown-automata route. A sufficiently long word accepted by empty stack drives the stack through a repeated configuration or to a great height, and in both cases the accepting computation is cut into segments that pump. The theorem is stated purely about an arbitrary pushdown automaton and its empty-stack language: grammars are never mentioned. Two ingredients are central: a normalization procedure which ensures that every step changes the stack height by one, preserving the whole language, the empty word included; and a well-founded recursion on the computation length that secures a non-empty pumped factor.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '12:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 12:30', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 11', title: 'A Machine-Checked Formalization of the Greibach Normal Form Conversion for Context-Free Grammars', authors: 'Rodrigo Ribeiro', abstract: 'The Greibach Normal Form (GNF) is a canonical form for context-free grammars in which every production begins with a terminal symbol. Converting an arbitrary grammar to GNF is a subtle process, which proceeds through a chain of transformations that eliminate ÃƒÆ’Ã…Â½Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Âµ-productions, unit rules, useless symbols, and left recursion before the final GNF step. Textbook presentations of this conversion usually argue its termination informally, leaving the underlying measure implicit. In this work, we present a formalization of the GNF conversion in which each stage carries a constructive proof that it preserves the language of the grammar it transforms. Composing these proofs yields an end-to-end theorem: every context-free grammar admits an equivalent grammar in Greibach Normal Form. All of our results are formalized using the Agda proof assistant.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '12:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 14:00', activity: 'Lunch Break', type: 'break' },
  { time: '14:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 15:30', activity: 'Session 2 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â Proof Repair and Formal Verification', chair: 'To be assigned', type: 'session' },
  { time: '14:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 14:30', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 5', title: 'CAPRI: Contract-Aware Proof Repair for Isabelle', authors: 'Jim Woodcock, Gabriel Leite, Augusto Sampaio, Ran Wei', abstract: 'We address the use of large language models (LLMs) to help discover Isabelle proofs. An Isabelle build establishes that the submitted theory is accepted, but not that an LLM changed only what the developer authorised. We present CAPRI, a contract-aware repair workflow in which Isabelle checks the proof and an independent checker enforces a machine-readable edit contract. Prompts, proposals, candidate repositories, diagnostics, verdicts, and hashes are retained for audit. We evaluate five workflows on twelve failed proofs from four developments, with three replicates per task and condition, giving 180 runs and 138 valid repairs. Of 144 terminal candidates accepted by Isabelle, six had modified protected text; all arose in iterative workflows that could edit a complete theory. A proof-body-only interface produced 29/36 valid repairs and no contract violations, compared with 31/36 for the corresponding full-theory workflow. One-shot repair produced 22/36, while a later prospectively frozen iterative workflow produced 32/36; these figures compare complete workflows rather than individual mechanisms.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '14:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 15:00', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 7', title: 'Preservation and Repair of Reachability Strategies in a Changing Game Arena', authors: 'Shobhit Singh, Qais Hamarneh, Mattias Ulbrich, Maike Schwammberger', abstract: 'Reachability games are a standard model for systems that must achieve a goal despite an uncooperative environment. Solving the game yields a winning strategy, and that strategy is deployed as the system\'s controller. Real systems, however, keep changing, and re-solving the game takes linear time in the size of the arena and produces a new controller while discarding one that is often still correct. We ask when a change preserves the strategy already in hand. A change is modelled as one of eight atomic edits, each carrying conditions on where it applies. These atomic changes can be composed to model a bigger change. For each edit we give a preservation test, in most cases a constant-time one. An edit may also break the strategy, either leaving it inapplicable, a syntactic break, or leaving it applicable but losing, a semantic break. We separate the two failures and repair syntactic breaks in constant time, apart from one case that is linear in the worst case, when a cheap repair exists.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '15:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 15:30', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 13', title: 'Mechanised Reasoning about SystemVerilog Assertions in Rocq', authors: 'Felipe Santos, Matheus Fonseca, Gustavo Carvalho', abstract: 'Contemporary RTL designs are inherently complex and, thus, they are increasingly difficult to validate and verify by simulation alone. Therefore, formal methods are typically employed to verify properties of interest, commonly represented as SystemVerilog Assertions (SVA). Many tools typically address the verification of SVA properties by employing solvers to analyse automata derived from SVA properties. However, due to the associated complexity and many domain-specific optimisations, it is not straightforward to demonstrate the correctness of such translation algorithms in general. In this work, we provide a foundational formalisation in the Rocq theorem prover of SVA expressions, waveforms (simulations), and automata for RTL design verification. Accompanied by domain-specific proof automation tactics we enable the construction of proof arguments regarding the semantic equivalence of SVA expressions and automata verification. Our contributions were validated considering an LLM-based pipeline, as well as a commercial tool (Xcelium). The developed automation tactics successfully discharged all valid proof goals. Tool support is also offered to translate waveforms and graphical representations of automata to our Rocq formalisation.', type: 'paper' },
