  { time: '16:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 17:30', activity: 'Session 3 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â Foundations and Formal Semantics', chair: 'To be assigned', type: 'session' },
  { time: '16:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 16:30', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 4', title: 'A Nominal Formalization of the Confluence of a Calculus with Explicit Substitutions via the Z Property', authors: 'FlÃƒÆ’Ã†â€™Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â¡vio Leonardo Cavalcanti de Moura', abstract: 'The Z property is a compact proof technique for confluence that has been used to give elegant confluence proofs for the ÃƒÆ’Ã…Â½Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â»-calculus and several of its extensions. Previous formalizations of the (compositional) Z property, as well as of the Substitution Lemma for calculi with explicit substitutions, were developed in Rocq. In this work, we recast this framework in a nominal setting, where variables are represented by atoms and binders are handled through name-swapping rather than de Bruijn indices, and formally verify it in the Rocq prover (version 9.1.1). As an application of this nominal framework, we formalize the ÃƒÆ’Ã…Â½Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â»x-calculus, an extension of the ÃƒÆ’Ã…Â½Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â»-calculus with explicit substitutions, and give a fully mechanized proof of its confluence via the compositional Z property. The whole development comprises around 4500 lines of Rocq code and 150 lemmas and theorems, and provides a reusable nominal toolkit for confluence proofs of calculi with explicit substitutions.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '16:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 17:00', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 12', title: 'Taming Paraconsistent Transition Semantics', authors: 'Rodrigo Alves, Juliana Cunha, Alexandre Madeira', abstract: 'Paraconsistent Labelled Transition Systems (PLTS) are structures in which transitions are weighted by a pair of values: one representing evidence in favour of the transition occurring and the other evidence against it. This framework naturally accommodates scenarios involving vague or inconsistent information, such as sensor-based control systems or medical decision-support systems based on conflicting examinations. While preserving contradictory information is often desirable at the modelling level, implementation and verification tasks ultimately require concrete bivalent outcomes. From this perspective, the expressive power of PLTS must be "tamed", bridging the gap between rich paraconsistent specifications and executable implementations. Motivated by such challenge, this paper introduces three forms of taming: at the level of truth values, models, and the satisfaction relation. We establish the relationships between these approaches and prove their equivalence for the diamond fragment of the modal logic. The proposed techniques have also been incorporated into the PLTS Toolkit, providing automated support for the corresponding modelling, design, and verification tasks.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '17:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 17:30', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 14', title: 'Program Derivation with Relations, Crescents, Bendy Loops and Colored Wires', authors: 'Gustavo Freire, Hugo Musso Gualandi, Hugo Nobrega, JoÃƒÆ’Ã†â€™Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â£o PaixÃƒÆ’Ã†â€™Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â£o', abstract: 'Program derivations calculate, via step-by-step reasoning, an algorithm which implements a given specification. In practice, however, the effectiveness of calculational methods depends on the mathematical language in which they are expressed, and known techniques tend to be verbose. As a way to make calculations more approachable and easier to discover, we study the application of a graphical language of string diagrams to the process of program derivation. By having diagrams represent feasibility relations, the notion of a Galois connection, which specifies functions as computing solutions to an optimization problem, becomes very simple to represent. This enables elegant point-free specifications, as well as proofs and program derivations that are built step-by-step from equalities between diagrams. We discuss how to represent derivational techniques graphically, and propose a novel point-free induction principle suited for string diagrams. For graphical induction we need to encode additional typing information in the diagrams, which we represent using colored wires. Finally, we showcase the methodology by deriving correct-by-construction recursive algorithms for problems on natural numbers and lists.', type: 'paper' },
