  { time: '10:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 10:30', activity: 'Coffee Break', type: 'break' },
  { time: '10:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 11:30', activity: 'Session 5 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â Tooling for Cyber-Physical Systems', chair: 'To be assigned', type: 'session' },
  { time: '10:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 11:00', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 6', title: 'Towards a translation-free model checking approach for SysML v2 actions', authors: 'Vitor Barros, Pedro Mesquita, Lucas Lima, Ciprian Teodorov', abstract: 'SysML v2 was designed with formal, executable semantics grounded in the Kernel Modeling Language (KerML), yet current tooling, including the official Pilot Implementation, provides no native engine to execute or verify these semantics, limiting engineers to static model authoring and leaving dynamic behavior unverified. Existing verification approaches close this gap by translating SysML v2 models into external formalisms, a strategy that introduces semantic gaps, proof obligations, and diagnostic results disconnected from the original model. This paper presents a translation-free approach to model checking SysML v2 actions, built on the GÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢Ãƒâ€¹Ã¢â‚¬Â ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬minÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢Ãƒâ€¹Ã¢â‚¬Â Ãƒâ€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢ framework, in which a Semantic Language Interface exposes a model\'s execution semantics directly as a transition system that the OBP verification engine explores without any intermediate transformation. We describe a proof-of-concept Java prototype that integrates the SysML v2 Pilot Implementation\'s parser with the OBP3 verification engine, which supports property specification in the Generic Property Specification Language over Linear Temporal Logic. We evaluate the approach on two case studies: a battery-charging system, in which a safety property violation is detected and traced directly to the responsible action, and a Close Air Support mission model, in which a structural defect causes a deadlock that is detected as a liveness property violation. Both cases demonstrate that counterexamples map directly onto the original SysML v2 elements, enabling engineers to reason about verification failures without artifacts introduced by translation.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '11:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 11:30', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 8', title: 'Edit, Check, Simulate and Train: A Toolkit for Urban Multi-lane Spatial Logic', authors: 'Qais Hamarneh, Nils Wenninghoff, Akhila Bairy, Mattis Bihler, Florian Portscher, Tim Kreuch, Tao Schmidt, Maike Schwammberger', abstract: 'Formal verification is crucial for ensuring the safety of autonomous vehicles, yet a significant gap remains between abstract spatial logics and executable, trainable environments. In this paper, we bridge this gap by introducing an extensive toolkit that operationalises Urban Multi-Lane Spatial Logic (UMLSL). We present two new tools, UMLSL-Edit and UMLSL-Sim. UMLSL-Edit is a visual traffic editor and satisfaction checker for UMLSL, enabling (i) generation of traffic scenarios with an arbitrary number of roads, intersections, and cars, (ii) formulation of UMLSL queries, and (iii) deciding whether a traffic scenario satisfies these queries, by a procedure we prove sound and, on a delimited fragment, complete. Scenarios created in UMLSL-Edit can be used as an input for UMLSL-Sim, a traffic simulation tool in which cars drive according to implementations of timed-automata-based traffic controllers that were proven safe, in the abstract model, in prior work. UMLSL-Sim is developed as a Gymnasium environment with a Reinforcement Learning (RL) implementation, allowing the user to (i) train RL agents whose reward is shaped by the safety controller, so that actions the controller rejects are penalised, (ii) set up different reward profiles to optimise training for different objectives, and (iii) observe how the RL agents respond to different scenarios created in UMLSL-Edit. The toolkit is open source and available on Github.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '11:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 14:00', activity: 'Lunch Break', type: 'break' },
  { time: '14:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 15:30', activity: 'Industrial Panel', chair: 'Moderator: To be assigned', type: 'session' },
  { time: '14:00 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 14:30', activity: 'Paper presentation', number: 'Paper 1', title: 'Data validation based on a formal specification of the safety requirements ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â 20 years of industrial experience', authors: 'Thierry Lecomte, Dalay Almeida, Vincent Germain, Michael Leuschel', abstract: 'Railway signalling systems are configured for each railway line by large volumes of constant data describing the track topology, the position of signals and switches, and the speed profiles. This data is as safety-critical as the certified software it parameterises: a single incorrect value can lead otherwise correct software to make an unsafe decision. Validating it has traditionally relied on cross-checking the configuration against reference track plans and regulations, an iterative activity that is slow, costly, inherently incomplete, and that must be repeated whenever the data changes. This paper reports on twenty years of industrial experience, at CLEARSY, with an alternative approach: formal data validation. The safety and consistency requirements that the data must satisfy are captured as a formal model in the mathematical language of the B method, and the conformance of the actual data against this model is then verified automatically, today by the ProB model checker, which either confirms a requirement over the whole data set or refutes it with an explicit counterexample. We retrace the evolution of the supporting tooling, from the early PredicateB evaluator to the ProB-based CLEARSY Data Solver and CLEARSY Data Manager; we describe the layered rule language built on accessors, intermediate constructs, and rules; and we discuss how the approach supports compliance with the railway safety standards, recalling that a national accident investigation bureau has explicitly recommended the use of formal methods for the validation of configuration data following an over-speed incident.', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '14:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 15:30', activity: 'Panel Discussion', description: 'Open discussion on industrial applications of formal methods ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â· Panellists: TBC', type: 'paper' },
  { time: '15:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 16:00', activity: 'Coffee Break', type: 'break' },
  { time: '16:30 ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œ 17:30', activity: 'Steering Committee', description: 'Business Meeting ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â open to all attendees', type: 'ceremony' },
